WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.891
Picture a restaurant that wants to know which of two cooks makes the house soup faster.

2
00:00:07.891 --> 00:00:14.215
The manager hands each cook an order, starts a stopwatch, and stops it when the bowl lands on the counter.

3
00:00:14.215 --> 00:00:15.051
Simple enough.

4
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:17.401
But one cook notices something.

5
00:00:17.401 --> 00:00:19.604
Nobody ever eats the test bowls.

6
00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:21.669
So the cook stops making them.

7
00:00:21.669 --> 00:00:23.390
The stopwatch reads zero.

8
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.836
Fastest soup in town.

9
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.553
Here's a question we rarely ask out loud.

10
00:00:27.553 --> 00:00:32.525
If I time my code with the clock, isn't the number on the clock simply the answer?

11
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.133
Let's try it.

12
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:38.721
Here's the benchmark almost everyone writes first.

13
00:00:38.721 --> 00:00:43.744
A benchmark is a small program that measures how long some code takes.

14
00:00:43.744 --> 00:00:44.821
Read the clock.

15
00:00:44.821 --> 00:00:50.347
Call Math.sqrt, a square root, 100 million times, and throw each result away.

16
00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:52.715
Read the clock again, and divide.

17
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:56.676
The lesson's NanoLoop runs that loop five times.

18
00:00:56.676 --> 00:01:02.825
Next to it, a second loop does the same work, but adds every result to a sum, and prints the sum.

19
00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:05.424
So, how long should one square root take?

20
00:01:05.424 --> 00:01:06.439
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:16.625
Round one: the loop that throws the result away says 0.07 nanoseconds a call.

22
00:01:16.625 --> 00:01:19.521
The loop that uses it says 3.4.

23
00:01:19.521 --> 00:01:21.763
Round two: 0.2, and 3.0.

24
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:24.442
And from round three on: 0.0000.

25
00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:26.995
Zero nanoseconds per square root.

26
00:01:26.995 --> 00:01:28.232
That's not fast.

27
00:01:28.232 --> 00:01:29.857
That's a magic trick.

28
00:01:29.857 --> 00:01:38.597
The used loop stays near 3 nanoseconds, which is believable for a square root and an addition, on a busy machine.

29
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.270
So where did the work go?

30
00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:49.342
Once C2, the JIT's optimizing compiler, had compiled the loop, it proved that nobody used the result of Math.sqrt.

31
00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:50.970
So it deleted the call.

32
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.369
Then the loop was empty, so it deleted the loop.

33
00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:59.608
That's called dead-code elimination: work whose result nobody uses can go.

34
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:01.434
Let's map it.

35
00:02:01.434 --> 00:02:03.235
The stopwatch is nanoTime.

36
00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:05.175
The test bowl is the result.

37
00:02:05.175 --> 00:02:08.431
Nobody eating it is our loop, throwing it away.

38
00:02:08.431 --> 00:02:11.340
And the cook who stops making bowls is C2.

39
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.420
But here the picture breaks, in a useful way.

40
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.035
A cook who skips the bowl knows they're cutting a corner.

41
00:02:18.035 --> 00:02:19.938
The JIT isn't cheating at all.

42
00:02:19.938 --> 00:02:24.884
Removing unused work is exactly its job, and our benchmark gave it permission.

43
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.124
And dead code is only one way a hand-made loop lies.

44
00:02:29.124 --> 00:02:36.858
If the input is a constant, C2 can work out the answer before the loop even runs, and you time the loading of one number.

45
00:02:36.858 --> 00:02:43.505
That's constant folding: the cook who gets the same order every day, and makes it before the doors open.

46
00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:49.867
The first rounds run in the interpreter, and in loop code compiled mid-run, as lesson 1 showed.

47
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:52.643
So round one measures the cook's first day.

48
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:54.837
And the JIT reshapes loops.

49
00:02:54.837 --> 00:03:04.251
JMH's own sample on loops reports an addition that seems to take one twentieth of a nanosecond, well beyond what hardware can do.

50
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:10.947
Now the worse case: a broken benchmark that prints a believable number.

51
00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:13.715
Swap the square root for Math.log.

52
00:03:13.715 --> 00:03:17.704
On JDK 21, the throw-away loop was never removed.

53
00:03:17.704 --> 00:03:21.611
It said 10 to 13 nanoseconds, round after round.

54
00:03:21.611 --> 00:03:22.670
Looks honest.

55
00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.809
And on JDK 25?

56
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.889
0.0000 by round three, like the square root.

57
00:03:27.889 --> 00:03:30.346
Same source, two JDKs, two stories.

58
00:03:30.346 --> 00:03:34.489
The magician got better, and nothing in your code told you.

59
00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:41.509
Whether the JIT can remove a call depends on what it can prove, and you can't see that from outside.

60
00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:43.671
So here's the rule.

61
00:03:43.671 --> 00:03:48.230
A timing loop you wrote by hand measures whatever the JIT left behind.

62
00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.728
Don't trust its number.

63
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:53.239
What we really want is a fair taste test.

64
00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:57.178
Warm the kitchen up first, and throw away the practice dishes.

65
00:03:57.178 --> 00:03:59.657
Use ingredients the cook can't predict.

66
00:03:59.657 --> 00:04:01.436
Have a judge eat every dish.

67
00:04:01.436 --> 00:04:03.851
And start each day in a fresh kitchen.

68
00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:09.753
That's what JMH does: the Java Microbenchmark Harness, from the OpenJDK team.

69
00:04:09.753 --> 00:04:13.151
You write a method and mark it as a benchmark.

70
00:04:13.151 --> 00:04:18.099
JMH writes the measuring loop around it, and runs it in fresh JVMs.

71
00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:21.349
Inputs live in fields the JIT can't predict.

72
00:04:21.349 --> 00:04:24.303
And whatever you return, the judge eats.

73
00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:31.545
Here's an honest benchmark for the report service's reading time, in Java and in Kotlin.

74
00:04:31.545 --> 00:04:34.343
words is a plain field, not a constant.

75
00:04:34.343 --> 00:04:39.579
And the result is returned, so JMH consumes it, and C2 has to compute it.

76
00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:44.887
In Kotlin the class must be open, because JMH's generated code extends it.

77
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.866
One warning about every number in this video.

78
00:04:48.866 --> 00:04:50.909
They came from a busy machine.

79
00:04:50.909 --> 00:04:54.384
Read the shape, zero against three, not the digits.

80
00:04:54.384 --> 00:04:59.629
And keep JMH's short demo runs for spotting traps, not for deciding anything.

81
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:06.279
The moral of the story: the JIT deletes work nobody uses, and folds work it can predict.

82
00:05:06.279 --> 00:05:10.393
A stopwatch around your loop can't tell you which one happened.

83
00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:14.441
So use the result, hide the input, and let JMH build the loop.

84
00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:15.491
Quick check.

85
00:05:15.491 --> 00:05:22.081
A loop takes the square root of 2.0, a constant, adds every result to a sum, and prints the sum.

86
00:05:22.081 --> 00:05:25.033
Is that an honest measure of a square root?

87
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:28.416
No.

88
00:05:28.416 --> 00:05:36.064
The result is used, but the input is a constant, so C2 can work out the square root once, before the loop.

89
00:05:36.064 --> 00:05:39.527
Next: two rules that fix most broken benchmarks.
